TODO
====

input

- synthetic release on window blur/occlusion. a drag whose release is lost
  to a mid-gesture visibility change leaves glass drag state (left or
  right button) dangling. a synthetic pointer-up at the last known position
  closes both. glass's drop-is-authoritative contract absorbs it cleanly.
  native half is a GLFW focus callback, browser half blur/visibilitychange.

protocol and platform

- resize should report tree space, not window space. with an in-window
  menubar the tree's layout space is smaller than the window by one row
  height, so fill-derived sizes are wrong server-side. report the space the
  tree lays out in, and re-report when a later menu op adds or removes the
  bar.

auth

the model: auth is app state, not framework machinery. mount a login tree,
check credentials in the handler, SetRoot to the real app. Authorize is for
apps that already have SSO or a proxy in front and want the socket refused
before it opens.

- optionally sign resume tokens so another instance behind a load balancer
  can tell "not mine" from "expired" instead of remounting silently.
- moving a live session between instances is not possible in the go sdk and
  probably never will be: a session is a closure graph.
  the recovery path is remount, with the app rebuilding from its own store. the
  clojure sdk is the one that could actually do it, since state is
  immutable data and the view is a pure function of it.
- what someone would actually build instead is affinity: find the instance
  already serving them and send them there. the bounce belongs on the host
  page request, not the socket, since browsers do not reliably follow
  redirects on a websocket handshake but the page load is ordinary http.
  the framework's only job is to not foreclose it: the resume token is an
  opaque random string today, and if an instance id were in it (or an app
  could supply a prefix) a load balancer could route on the query param
  alone. worth remembering whenever tokens get signed.
